{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Remember the sabbath day, to hallow it.</b> Since He warned concerning acceptance of the yoke of His kingship, blessed be He — that one have no other elohim — and adjoined to it the matter of the prohibition of oath-taking, for all this pertains to the sanctity of His name, blessed be He, He adjoined to them the remembering of the sabbath day: that all the days of the week one remember the sabbath day. For this reason they said in the Mekhilta: do not count as the way the other nations count, but rather count toward the name of Shabbat — 'first toward Shabbat,' 'second toward Shabbat.' And through this you shall remember the sabbath day, and this remembrance is effective for hallowing the name, blessed be He. And this is what He said, 'to hallow it' — for He did not say 'hallow the sabbath day,' since it is already hallowed and standing; rather, 'to hallow it' refers back to HaShem, blessed be He, mentioned above in 'You shall not take.' For through an oath one profanes the name, as it is written (Leviticus 19:12), ״וְלֹא תִשָּׁבְעוּ בִשְׁמִי לַשֶּׁקֶר וְחִלַּלְתָּ אֶת שֵׁם אֱלֹהֶיךָ״. And correspondingly, remembering the sabbath day is effective for you to hallow the name, blessed be He, through your remembering that in six days YHWH made the heavens and the earth by His name, blessed be He, as it is written (Isaiah 26:4), ״כִּי בְיָהּ ה׳ צוּר עוֹלָמִים״. And through this remembrance you will know to conduct yourself with holiness toward His name, blessed be He, when you remember that by His name, blessed be He, all the worlds were created — and if so, assuredly all of them depend upon His name, blessed be He." "And you shall know and understand what the mystics have written, that שְׁבוּעָה (oath) is derived from the word שִׁבְעָה (seven), as Rabbeinu Bachaye wrote in this parashah and in parashat Vayera on the verse ״עַל כֵּן קָרָא לַמָּקוֹם בְּאֵר שָׁבַע כִּי שָׁם נִשְׁבְּעוּ שְׁנֵיהֶם״ (Genesis 21:31). And this is because the Holy One, blessed be He, created all existent beings and bounded them with six extremities, and the great name, blessed be He, is the seventh, riding upon them all. And the one who swears touches the seventh — that is, His name, blessed be He. And Shabbat hallows the seventh; therefore it is fitting that the Holy One, blessed be He — the seventh — be hallowed by means of the seventh day, and those who understand will grasp this matter, for it is clear. And just as one does not make use of a name from among the holy names alluded to in the number seven, so too one may not engage in labor on the seventh day; therefore every seventh is holy to YHWH — whether in days, whether in the sabbatical year, whether in the Jubilee."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The Mekhilta citation 'do not count' paraphrases Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishma'el, Yitro, Babodesh 7; the verse from Isaiah 26:4 is cited with its well-known rabbinic reading that בְּיָהּ refers to creation through the two-letter divine name.", 1 "The Hebrew verb in 'do not make use' (אֵין מִשְׁתַּמְּשִׁים) carries the specific kabbalistic sense of ritual 'use' of divine names; rendered literally to preserve that resonance."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:51.107536126Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}